WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.570
We're going to take a look at back propagation.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:08.789
It's central to the functioning of neural networks, helping them to learn and adapt.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.720
And we're going to cover it in simple but instructive terms.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:21.329
So even if your only knowledge of neural networks is "Isn't that something to do with chatGPT?" Well, we've got you covered.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:27.682
Now, a neural network fundamentally comprises multiple layers of neurons interconnected by weights.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:36.450
So I'm going to draw some neurons here, and I'm organizing them in layers.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:39.450
And these neurons are also known as nodes.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.710
Now, the layers here are categorized.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.369
So that's let's do that, the categorization.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:50.039
We have a layer here called the input layer.

00:00:50.790 --> 00:01:00.735
These two layers in the middle here are the hidden layer and the layer on the end here, that is the output layer.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:06.689
And these neurons are all interconnected with each other across the layers.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:15.107
So each neuron is connected to each other neuron in the next layer.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:19.282
So you can see that here.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.920
Okay, so now we have our basic neural network.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:31.252
And during a process called forward propagation, the input data traverses through these layers where the weights,

00:01:31.252 --> 00:01:36.150
biases and activation functions transform the data until an output is produced.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.120
So, let's define those terms.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:44.730
Weights, what is that when we're talking about a neural network?

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:51.180
Well, the weights define the strength of the connections between each of the neurons.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:02:00.760
Then we have the activation function, and the activation function is applied to the weighted sum of the inputs

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:07.859
at each neuron to introduce non-linearity into the network, and that allows it to make complex relationships.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:12.133
And that's really where we can use activation functions.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.979
Commonly, you'll see activation functions used such as sigmoid, for example.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.853
And then finally, biases.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:28.739
So biases really are the additional parameter that shift the activation function to the left or the right, and that aids the network's flexibility.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:33.150
So, consider a single training instance with its associated input data.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:36.610
Now, this data propagates forward through the network,

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:41.999
causing every neutron to calculate a weighted sum of the inputs, which is then passed through its activation function.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.879
And the final result is the network's output.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:46.787
Great!

00:02:46.787 --> 00:02:50.400
So where does back propagation come in?

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:54.280
Well, the initial output might not be accurate.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:58.989
The network needs to learn from its mistakes and adjust its weights to improve.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:06.120
And back propagation is essentially an algorithm used to train neural networks, applying the principle of error correction.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:15.900
So, after forward propagation, the output error, which is the difference between the network's output and the actual output, is computed.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:22.180
Now that's something called a loss function.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:31.860
And the error is distributed back through the network, providing each neuron in the network a measure of its contribution to total error.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:38.219
Using these measures, back propagation adjusts the weights and the biases of the network to minimize that error.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:44.189
And the objective here is to improve the accuracy of the network's output during subsequent forward propagation.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:54.350
It's a process of optimization, often employing a technique known as gradient descent.

00:03:54.350 --> 00:04:00.889
Now, gradient descent, that's the topic of a whole video of its own,

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:01.880
but essentially, 

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:07.488
gradient descent is an algorithm used to find the optimal weights and biases that minimize the lost function.

00:04:07.488 --> 00:04:13.669
It iteratively adjusts the weights and biases in the direction that reduces the error most rapidly.

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:16.519
And that means the steepest descent.

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:20.629
Now, back propagation is widely used in many neural networks.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:23.299
So let's consider a speech recognition system.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:29.480
We provide as input a spoken word, and it outputs a written transcript of that word.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:38.869
Now, if during training our spoken inputs, it turns out that it doesn't match the written outputs, then back propagation may be able to help.

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:44.329
Look, I speak with a British accent, but I've lived in the US for years.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:05:01.220
But when locals here ask for my name-- Martin --they often hear it as something different entirely, like Marvin or Morton or Mark.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.340
If this neural network had made the same mistake, we'd calculate the error

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:14.090
by using the loss function to quantify the difference between the predicted output "Marvin" and the actual output "Martin".

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:20.161
We'd compute the gradient of the loss function with respect to the weight and biases in the network

00:05:20.161 --> 00:05:23.260
and update the weighting biases in the network accordingly.

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:29.480
Then we'd undergo multiple iterations of forward propagation and back propagation,

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:43.053
tinkering with those weights and biases until we reach convergence-- a time where the network could reliably translate Martin into M-A-R-T-I-N.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.249
This is can't be applied to people, can it?

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:50.893
Well, but anyway, let's just talk about one more thing with back propagation,

00:05:50.893 --> 00:05:57.169
and that's the distinction between static and recurrent back propagation networks.

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:01.160
Let's start with static.

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:05.722
So static back propagation is employed in a feed-forward neural networks

00:06:05.722 --> 00:06:10.009
where the data moves in a single direction from input layer to output layer.

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:14.611
Some example use cases of this, well, we can think of OCR,

00:06:14.611 --> 00:06:21.049
or optical character recognition, where the goal is to identify and classify the letters and numbers in a given image.

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:30.198
Another common example is with spam detection, and here we are looking to use a neural network

00:06:30.198 --> 00:06:39.589
to learn from features such as the emails, content and the sender's email address to classify an email as spam or not spam.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:50.090
Now back propagation can also be applied to recurrent neural networks as well, or RNNs.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:57.199
Now these networks have loops, and this type of back propagation is slightly more complex given the recursive nature of these networks.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:57.815
Now, some use cases?

00:06:57.815 --> 00:07:04.850
If we think about sentiment analysis, that's a common use case for this.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:12.049
And that's a good example of where RNNs are used to analyze the sentiment of a piece of text, like a customer product review.

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:19.340
Another good example is time series prediction.

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:24.062
So predicting things like stock prices or weather patterns.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:29.269
Ultimately, back propagation is the backbone of the learning in neural networks.

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:35.109
It tests for errors, working its way back from the output layer to the input layer,

00:07:35.109 --> 00:07:40.699
adjusting the weights as it goes with the goal to minimize future errors.

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:47.509
Errors like how to pronounce Martin in a passible American accent.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:53.658
MART-EN... MAR-EN... MART-ENNE.

